## Structured Insights Direction

Structured insights remain one of the strongest product decisions.

The app shifted from:

- AI-generated notes.

Toward:

- Actionable meeting intelligence.

The most valuable outputs remain:

- Action items.
- Decisions.
- Due dates.
- Ownership.
- Review warnings.
- Confidence scoring.
- Follow-up visibility.

Current design guidance:

- Insights should feel more important than raw transcript text.
- Raw transcript should remain available as evidence/context, not the primary product value.
- Insight cards should feel operational, not decorative.

## Current UX Risks

### Utility Panel Risk

There is still a risk the UI becomes too infrastructure-focused.

Users should feel:

- I captured a meeting and instantly received organized outcomes.

Users should NOT feel:

- I operated an AI audio processing pipeline.

Implementation details such as chunking, retry logic, WebSocket behavior, and processing internals should remain secondary unless troubleshooting is needed.

### Transcript Dominance Risk

Raw transcripts are useful but visually noisy.

Users believe they want transcripts. What they actually value most often is:

- Decisions.
- Tasks.
- Accountability.
- Follow-up.
- Searchable memory.

Transcript visibility should remain available but should not overpower actionable insights.

### Product Sophistication vs Visual Polish

The backend and product thinking are now sophisticated enough that visual polish needs to keep pace.

Continue refining:

- Typography hierarchy.
- Spacing consistency.
- Density control.
- Card hierarchy.
- Insight hierarchy.
- Window layout behavior.
- Theme consistency.

## Live Capture UX Review

Live Capture remains the most important near-term design opportunity.

Current assessment:

- Functionality is strong.
- Visual quality is good.
- The screen still risks feeling like a dashboard of panels instead of a guided meeting workflow.
- Too many areas compete equally for attention.

The screen currently has to balance:

- Meeting setup.
- Recording state.
- Timer.
- Audio levels.
- Transcript.
- Insights.
- Theme controls.
- Recording controls.

Current recommendation:

Refactor the experience toward clearer workflow zones.

Top section:

- Meeting title.
- Meeting profile / note template in a calmer setup area.
- Recording state.
- Elapsed time.

Middle section:

- Live transcript.
- Speaker activity.
- Realtime context.

Right rail:

- AI insights.
- Action items.
- Decisions.
- Dates.
- Risks / review warnings.

Bottom sticky control area:

- Record / Stop.
- Pause, if implemented.
- Add note.
- Mark important / bookmark.

The page should feel workflow-oriented rather than panel-oriented.

The user should always intuitively know:

- Current meeting state.
- What matters right now.
- What Nova has already identified.

